WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.490
- Another scientifically inaccurate

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.610
and potentially harmful statement

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.970
made by another celebrity about health.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:08.172
It's no surprise, but we're talking

00:00:08.172 --> 00:00:10.720
about it on this week's Wednesday checkup.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.303
(upbeat music)

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:17.530
I found out last week

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:20.300
that Lady Gaga sat down
to have an open and honest

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.114
conversation with Oprah
about her mental health.

00:00:23.114 --> 00:00:26.190
This conversation talked
about her own journey,

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.550
breaking the mental
health stigma that exists

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.111
and addressing disparities.

00:00:30.111 --> 00:00:32.830
Now all of those things
are incredibly important

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:34.130
and I'm actually really passionate

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.760
about addressing those issues.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.500
In fact, many of my
patients who are uninsured

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.153
or only have catastrophic insurance,

00:00:40.153 --> 00:00:42.730
they face a really tough task

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:44.820
in finding a mental health counselor,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.560
therapist, psychiatrist,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.130
and it's my job as a
family medicine doctor

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:50.960
to not only fill that void

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.580
but also help them navigate
this really tricky system

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.170
that we have here in the United States.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.090
When it comes to mental health stigma

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.648
this absolutely needs to be broken.

00:00:58.648 --> 00:01:00.770
But, in order to break
the mental health stigma

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.670
we need honest and accurate information.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.100
When we talk about someone having

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.685
a mental health issue, that's not to say

00:01:06.685 --> 00:01:08.266
that they're weak, it's not to say

00:01:08.266 --> 00:01:09.780
that there's something wrong with them.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.920
But diagnosing ourselves
is not the solution.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:14.017
Reading a book and saying,

00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:16.277
"Oh, I must have x, y, and z,

00:01:16.277 --> 00:01:17.698
"and now I need to self-treat myself,"

00:01:17.698 --> 00:01:19.630
also is not ideal.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:20.970
The most ideal way

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.490
to go about getting proper
mental health treatment

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.352
is, A, to see a licensed therapist,

00:01:25.352 --> 00:01:28.394
psychiatrist, or primary care physician.

00:01:28.394 --> 00:01:30.450
And I say primary care physician

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.900
because there was a specific

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.050
segment within Lady Gaga's speech

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:36.727
that is harmfully inaccurate.

00:01:36.727 --> 00:01:38.787
"By the way, if your primary care doctor

00:01:38.787 --> 00:01:40.757
"is prescribing you an antidepressant,

00:01:40.757 --> 00:01:42.367
"this should not be happening.

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:44.287
"Primary care doctors
should be introducing

00:01:44.287 --> 00:01:47.970
"you to a psychiatrist, an
expert in brain medication."

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.890
Now as a primary care doctor,

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.380
a board certified family
medicine physician,

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.720
my problem with this statement
is not personal offense.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.660
Lady Gaga did not offend me
by saying this statement.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.922
But what she is saying is
scientifically inaccurate

00:02:01.922 --> 00:02:03.910
and it's wrong, factually.

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:05.564
Let's start off on a practical note.

00:02:05.564 --> 00:02:07.520
Let's talk about the numbers.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:08.850
The National Institute of Health

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.800
estimates that one in five adults

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.210
will have a mental health issue.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:16.610
When I say issue, a classifiable
mental health disorder.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:20.410
That equates to about 40 to 66 million

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.230
individuals with a condition.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.063
Now there's only

00:02:23.063 --> 00:02:26.287
about 20,000 practicing
psychiatrists in the U.S.

00:02:26.287 --> 00:02:28.310
How are all those people gonna get care?

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:29.520
And on top of that,

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.480
there's certain geographic limitations

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.800
where there are no practicing
psychiatrists in a given area.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.550
Then there is people who have
lower socioeconomic standing

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.340
who are uninsured and have higher

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.285
rates of mental illness.

00:02:40.285 --> 00:02:42.660
What are they supposed to do, Lady Gaga?

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.730
Second, we don't wanna
put even more stress

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.222
on our overburdened
mental healthcare system.

00:02:48.222 --> 00:02:49.380
Think about this,

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.190
psychiatrists are
specialists in what they do

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:55.410
and they should be seeing
more complex cases.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.412
If every single time any patient

00:02:57.412 --> 00:02:59.360
that needed an antidepressant needed

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.430
to be referred to them,

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:02.720
think about the incredible wait times

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.730
that that would create for
patients who are truly complex

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.480
and can't be treated by
family medicine doctors.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:09.942
This will completely break

00:03:09.942 --> 00:03:12.180
our already nearly broken system.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.593
In fact, psychiatrists
and other specialists

00:03:14.593 --> 00:03:16.540
that I've worked with in the past

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.189
have sometimes laid the
complaint at my feet

00:03:19.189 --> 00:03:22.810
that some family medicine
providers are too quick to refer

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.780
instead of trying first line
treatments like antidepressants

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:27.299
and I completely agree with that

00:03:27.299 --> 00:03:29.344
which goes completely against

00:03:29.344 --> 00:03:31.950
what Lady Gaga was trying
to say in this speech.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:35.000
Three, board certified
family medicine doctors

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.701
receive extensive education and training

00:03:37.701 --> 00:03:41.470
in mental health conditions
and brain medications

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:44.290
with the ability to refer
or triage as needed.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.340
In reality, it's the actual job

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.010
of the family medicine doctor

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.040
to be able to administer first line

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.650
and even second line treatments

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:54.140
before sending the
patient to a specialist.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:56.211
If you try the first line
treatment, and it fails,

00:03:56.211 --> 00:03:58.930
maybe you try the second
line treatment, and it fails,

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:00.180
then you need a specialist.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.013
If you need to perform

00:04:01.013 --> 00:04:02.820
a procedure that you're not trained in

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.188
or you don't have the
equipment to perform,

00:04:04.188 --> 00:04:06.160
you need to send them to a specialist.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:07.842
If you've come across a complex case

00:04:07.842 --> 00:04:11.070
with a patient having
more than one illness

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.014
or taking more than one medication

00:04:13.014 --> 00:04:16.290
that could be high enough
complexity to warrant a referral.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:17.500
Perhaps you're not comfortable

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.475
with a specific illness
that the patient has

00:04:19.475 --> 00:04:21.480
and you don't have enough
experience with it.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.610
That warrants a referral

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.012
but these are the minority of cases.

00:04:24.012 --> 00:04:27.640
The huge majority of patients
with anxiety and depression

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.530
who warrant antidepressants

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.400
can and should be treated
by primary care doctors.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.530
Also, as a side note, not
every patient with anxiety

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.720
and depression needs antidepressants.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.225
And, in fact, that's the
beauty of family medicine.

00:04:40.225 --> 00:04:44.800
We understand that cognitive
behavioral therapy, DBT,

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.930
all these options work
very well for our patients

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.250
and because we have existing relationships

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.083
with these patients,

00:04:51.083 --> 00:04:52.954
because we're their primary care providers

00:04:52.954 --> 00:04:56.820
we treat them for years,
we know what works.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.291
We know what medications
they do well with.

00:04:59.291 --> 00:05:01.461
They have direct access to us

00:05:01.461 --> 00:05:03.640
and it only makes sense that we continue

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:05.860
this line of treatment until it passes

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.612
that complexity line where we do need

00:05:07.612 --> 00:05:10.210
to get a specialist like
a psychiatrist involved.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:12.840
Four, I think there's a faulty mentality

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:14.490
at the heart of this statement

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:18.864
where more money spent always
yields better care or outcomes

00:05:18.864 --> 00:05:20.710
and that's just not the case.

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:22.550
In reality, I have many celebrity

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:23.870
or wealthy patients that come

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:26.419
in to see me that
request every single test

00:05:26.419 --> 00:05:28.189
or every single treatment.

00:05:28.189 --> 00:05:29.740
And I have to explain to them

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:32.330
that tests come with possible risks.

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:34.410
Treatments come with possible risks.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:35.330
Even though some of them may

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:37.670
seem completely benign like a blood test

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.833
and we need to have those conversations.

00:05:39.833 --> 00:05:41.631
Now you may be wondering to yourself,

00:05:41.631 --> 00:05:44.460
how is seeing a specialist
putting me at risk?

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.197
Well, it's not directly
putting you at risk

00:05:46.197 --> 00:05:48.150
because specialists are very confident

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.050
they know what they're doing

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.150
but, from my experience,

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.440
what I've seen is, if a patient

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.260
who does not have a primary care doctor

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.270
gets sent to a specialist too early,

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:59.000
they sometimes get
treatments or even tests

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.690
for things that they don't need yet.

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:03.070
This is not because the specialists

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:04.010
don't know what they're doing

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:06.560
or because they're trying to
push things onto the patient.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.690
It's because they're used to
seeing more complex cases.

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:12.075
So they have a lower threshold

00:06:12.075 --> 00:06:14.910
of when to order certain
tests or certain treatments.

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.411
And they don't have existing relationships

00:06:16.411 --> 00:06:19.990
with these patients like we
do as primary care providers.

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:21.677
For example, I have some patients,

00:06:21.677 --> 00:06:23.010
and when they come in, they say that

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:24.970
their pain level is a four or a five,

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:25.940
I get really worried

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:26.950
because I already know that

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:28.122
they have a very high pain threshold.

00:06:28.122 --> 00:06:29.760
But if they go see a cardiologist

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.120
and they say, "Oh, my pain is a four."

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:32.940
Maybe it's not taken as seriously.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:34.598
If you jump onto any review site

00:06:34.598 --> 00:06:36.420
you'll see patients complaining

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.755
that they didn't get tests or
treatments from their doctor

00:06:38.755 --> 00:06:40.760
and they think that's a bad thing.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.353
But I recently came across a tweet

00:06:42.353 --> 00:06:44.542
from a doctor, Christina Johnson,

00:06:44.542 --> 00:06:46.980
that I think works very well in this case.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:49.037
It reads, "Just because no tests

00:06:49.037 --> 00:06:50.957
"were performed or medicines prescribed

00:06:50.957 --> 00:06:52.958
"it doesn't mean that
you didn't get care."

00:06:52.958 --> 00:06:54.710
I completely agree with this statement

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:56.795
because it takes careful evaluation

00:06:56.795 --> 00:06:58.530
to know what a patient has.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:01.310
To weight the risks and
benefits of each procedure

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.130
and each treatment, and figure out

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:05.500
what's right for the individual
sitting in front of you.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:08.757
That's why generalizations
don't work well in medicine.

00:07:08.757 --> 00:07:11.184
And that's why Lady Gaga's statement here

00:07:11.184 --> 00:07:14.140
does more harm than good
at the end of the day.

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:15.403
It puts patients at risk

00:07:15.403 --> 00:07:17.340
who would otherwise go get care

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.140
from a family medicine doctor.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.540
It hurts existing
doctor-patient relationships

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:24.016
that are potentially going very well.

00:07:24.016 --> 00:07:25.340
I guess the final thing

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:26.480
that really makes this statement

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:29.590
inaccurate is that pretty much
every medical organization

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:31.635
agrees that family medicine doctors

00:07:31.635 --> 00:07:34.360
are more than capable and are trained

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.289
to prescribe antidepressants, to know

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:39.012
when to refer patients
if a complexity arises.

00:07:39.012 --> 00:07:42.321
And to make a universal
statement like that

00:07:42.321 --> 00:07:44.580
that this agrees with all of medicine.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.040
It just doesn't make a lot of sense.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:47.020
Just four days ago,

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:49.560
the active president
of the AFP, Dr. Leroy,

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:50.547
made a great statement.

00:07:50.547 --> 00:07:51.877
"Family medicine physicians

00:07:51.877 --> 00:07:54.700
"are on the front lines of
mental health care in America."

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:56.470
And I completely agree
with that statement.

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:57.303
Do you know why?

00:07:57.303 --> 00:07:58.954
I would actually go as far as to say

00:07:58.954 --> 00:08:03.954
80% of my visits, there is a
mental healthcare component.

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:06.129
So when a patient comes in for back pain,

00:08:06.129 --> 00:08:10.590
acid reflux, diabetes,
chronic care management,

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.740
those usually have a mental
health component in them.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.220
Whether we see it or not,

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:16.960
whether we diagnose it or not,

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.140
and if we're gonna go ahead
and make a universal statement

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.607
and say, "Family medicine doctors

00:08:20.607 --> 00:08:22.267
"should prescribe antidepressants

00:08:22.267 --> 00:08:25.020
"or they're incapable to
take care of mental health."

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:26.070
It's just flat out wrong.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.290
I'm dropping some great
resources down below,

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:29.320
including a response

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:31.270
from the American Academy
of Family Physicians

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:33.161
and a fantastic open letter

00:08:33.161 --> 00:08:35.550
by Dr. Megan Babb on the subject.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:36.574
I encourage you to read it.

00:08:36.574 --> 00:08:38.066
A lot of the same points I brought up

00:08:38.066 --> 00:08:40.540
in this video where addressed
in her letter as well.

00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:42.900
And we really send a message to Lady Gaga

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.370
and really all influential
celebrities out there.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:48.315
Your message is gonna be
heard by millions of people.

00:08:48.315 --> 00:08:51.185
They're gonna trust you
because of your familiar face.

00:08:51.185 --> 00:08:53.000
They have respect for you.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:54.120
So if you're gonna be making

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.223
universal recommendations
about healthcare,

00:08:56.223 --> 00:08:59.259
please, please, please, partner
with a medical organization,

00:08:59.259 --> 00:09:01.620
partner with a medical professional

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:05.050
before more harm than good is
done with those statements.

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:05.970
If you're interested to watch

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:07.780
some of my other videos
about mental health

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:09.320
which I'm very passionate about

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:12.685
as an osteopathic and holistic
physician, click here.

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:15.316
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:09:15.316 --> 00:09:17.983
(upbeat music)

